      "content_text": "# Articles (exceptions)\n\n**Level**: Class 9-10 | **Topic**: Articles: Exceptions (আর্টিকেলের ব্যতিক্রমী নিয়ম)\n\n\n## 1. 📖 Grammar Rules & Explanations (নিয়মাবলী ও ব্যাখ্যা)\n\n- **Review the basics:** 'a' or 'an' for one nonspecific item (a book, an umbrella) and 'the' for something someone already knows about (the book we read yesterday).\n\n- **Use 'the' for unique things or well-known places:** the sun, the President, the Prime Minister, the Bay of Bengal.\n\n- Zero article (no 'a' or 'the') works with plural general ideas (Students love festivals) and most country names (Bangladesh is beautiful) or meals (We ate lunch).\n\n- **Special cases:** Use 'the' with rivers, oceans, mountain ranges, newspapers, and some countries (the Nile, the Pacific, the Himalayas, the Daily Star, the United Kingdom).\n\n- **Reminder for learners:** Think of your day at school or the market. If you talk about a new item you saw, say 'a'. When you mention the same item again or a known place, use 'the'.\n\n- **Decide between 'a' and 'an' by sound:** use 'an' before vowel sounds (an apple, an hour, an honour) and 'a' before consonant sounds even if the first letter is a vowel (a university, a one-time event).\n\n- Use 'a/an' with singular, countable nouns when you introduce them (a book, an umbrella), but drop the article for plural countables or uncountables when speaking generally (Books are expensive, Water is precious). Use 'some' for unspecified plural/uncountable references.\n\n- Choose 'the' before superlatives, ordinals, institutions, and when you talk about what everybody knows (the best student, the first prize, the university, the older brother).\n\n- Remember that proper nouns usually skip the article unless they refer to groups, rivers, oceans, or are plural (the Himalayas, the Danube, the United States) or include words like 'Republic' or 'Kingdom'.\n\n\n## 2. 💡 Practical Examples (বাস্তব উদাহরণসমূহ)\n\n1. The students read the book they borrowed from the library. (First mention: a book? Later: the book.)\n\n2. Bangladesh has hot summers. (Country name does not need an article.)\n\n3. He bought an orange, and the orange was sweet. (First a/an, then the.)\n\n4. The Buriganga flows through Dhaka. (River takes 'the'.)\n\n5. Cats are playful animals. (General plural, so no article.)\n\n\n## 3. ✍️ Practice Drills (অনুশীলনমূলক কাজ)\n\n- **Exercise 1:** Explain why 'the' appears in 'the National Parliament building'.\n\n- **Exercise 2:** Fill in blanks: I saw ___ kite in the sky. ___ kite was red.\n\n- **Exercise 3:** Choose the right article for: ___ Ganges, ___ freedom, ___ farmer.\n\n- **Exercise 4:** Describe when to skip an article: ___ students studied for ___ exam.\n\n- **Exercise 5:** Which article fits: ___ honest boy, ___ university event, ___ hour-long class?\n\n- **Exercise 6:** Explain why we say 'Rice is served' without an article but 'the rice you cooked' when you point to specific rice.",
