      "content_text": "# Voice (Active and Passive)\n\n**Level**: Class 9-10 | **Topic**: Voice: Active & Passive (বাচ্য পরিবর্তন: সম্পূর্ণ নিয়ম)\n\n\n## 1. 📖 Grammar Rules & Explanations (নিয়মাবলী ও ব্যাখ্যা)\n\n- **Voice:** The relationship between the verb and the subject. This is very important for SSC/HSC exams!\nActive Voice: Subject performs the action. (Who did it? - Focus on the doer)\nExample: The students cleaned the classroom.\nPassive Voice: Subject receives the action. (What happened? - Focus on the action)\nExample: The classroom was cleaned by the students.\n💡 Student Tip: In passive voice, move the object to the front and use 'by' before the subject!\n\n- **Active to Passive Voice Transformation:** Active: Subject + Verb + Object\nPassive: Object + was/were/is/are + past participle + by + Subject\nExample: Rahim writes a letter. → A letter is written by Rahim.\nExample: They will build the Padma Bridge. → The Padma Bridge will be built by them.\n💡 Remember: Use correct form of 'be' (is/am/are/was/were) + past participle (V3)\n\n- **Present Tense Active to Passive:** Active: He eats an apple. → Passive: An apple is eaten by him.\nActive: They play football. → Passive: Football is played by them.\nActive: She teaches English. → Passive: English is taught by her.\n\n- **Past Tense Active to Passive:** Active: The students wrote an essay. → Passive: An essay was written by the students.\nActive: They built the Padma Bridge. → Passive: The Padma Bridge was built by them.\nActive: She bought a new saree from Dhaka. → Passive: A new saree was bought by her from Dhaka.\n💡 Past tense: use 'was' or 'were' + V3\n\n- **Future Tense Active to Passive:** Active: He will complete the SSC homework. → Passive: The SSC homework will be completed by him.\nActive: She will solve the math problem. → Passive: The math problem will be solved by her.\nActive: Bangladesh Cricket Team will win the match. → Passive: The match will be won by Bangladesh Cricket Team.\n💡 Future: use 'will be' + V3\n\n- **When to use Passive Voice (Important for exam writing!):** ✓ Focus on the action or result (not who did it)\n✓ When the agent is unknown: The museum was robbed.\n✓ When the agent is obvious: He was arrested (by police).\n✓ In formal or scientific writing: The experiment was conducted carefully.\n✓ When the agent is less important: The school was damaged in the storm.\n\n- **Perfect tenses in passive:** Combine have/has/had + been + past participle to keep the sense of completion without naming the actor.\nExample: Active: She has completed the task. → Passive: The task has been completed by her.\nActive: They had studied the course. → Passive: The course had been studied by them.\n\n- **Modal verbs in passive use modal + be + past participle to express possibility, permission, or obligation while keeping the object as the focus.\nExample:** Active: You can submit the report. → Passive: The report can be submitted by you.\nActive: He must deliver the speech. → Passive: The speech must be delivered by him.\nExample: Active: They should finish the project. → Passive: The project should be finished by them.\n\n- **Leave out the agent when it is unknown or irrelevant; include it with 'by' when you must mention the doer.\nExample:** Active: People admire her talent. → Passive: Her talent is admired (by people).\nUse the passive in academic and formal writing to stress the action or outcome rather than the performer.\n\n\n## 2. 💡 Practical Examples (বাস্তব উদাহরণসমূহ)\n\n1. Active: The teacher teaches English grammar in Dhaka school. → Passive: English grammar is taught by the teacher in Dhaka school.\n\n2. Active: She bought hilsa fish from the market. → Passive: Hilsa fish was bought by her from the market.\n\n3. Active: They will host the debate competition. → Passive: The debate competition will be hosted by them.\n\n4. Active: The police caught the thief in Chittagong. → Passive: The thief was caught by the police in Chittagong.\n\n5. Active: Someone stole my textbook. → Passive: My textbook was stolen (agent unknown).\n\n6. Active: Bangladeshi students respect their teachers. → Passive: Teachers are respected by Bangladeshi students.\n\n7. Active: Scientists discovered a new species in Sundarbans. → Passive: A new species was discovered by scientists in Sundarbans.\n\n8. Active: Nazrul has written many poems. → Passive: Many poems have been written by Nazrul.\n\n\n## 3. ✍️ Practice Drills (অনুশীলনমূলক কাজ)\n\n- **Exercise 1:** Convert to passive: 'She paints beautiful pictures.'\n\n- **Exercise 2:** Convert to passive: 'The company built this bridge.'\n\n- **Exercise 3:** Convert to passive: 'Someone has stolen the jewels.'\n\n- **Exercise 4:** Convert to passive: 'They will announce the results tomorrow.'\n\n- **Exercise 5:** Convert to active: 'The letter was written by him.'\n\n- **Exercise 6:** Identify the voice: 'The book was published by the author.'",
