      "content_text": "# Word Order & Emphasis\n\n**Level**: Class 11-12 | **Topic**: Word Order & Emphasis (শব্দের ক্রম ও ইনভার্সন)\n\n\n## 1. 📖 Grammar Rules & Explanations (নিয়মাবলী ও ব্যাখ্যা)\n\n- **🎓 Word Order & Emphasis:** Master advanced sentence structures for HSC! Learn to emphasize important information using special word order patterns.\n\n- **Cleft Sentences with 'It is...that/who':** Used to emphasize a particular part of a sentence.\nNormal: Karim won the prize.\nEmphasis on Subject: It was Karim who won the prize.\nEmphasis on Object: It was the prize that Karim won.\nEmphasis on Time: It was yesterday that Karim won the prize.\n💡 HSC Tip: Use 'who' for people, 'that' for things!\n\n- **Cleft Sentences with 'What...is/was':** Emphasizes actions or objects.\nNormal: She needs confidence.\nEmphasis: What she needs is confidence.\nNormal: He said something strange.\nEmphasis: What he said was strange.\nNormal: I want success in HSC.\nEmphasis: What I want is success in HSC.\n💡 'What' clauses emphasize the noun/action!\n\n- **Fronting (Moving to Front):** Moving important information to the beginning for emphasis.\nNormal: She achieved success through hard work.\nFronting: Through hard work, she achieved success.\nNormal: I have never seen such dedication.\nFronting: Never have I seen such dedication. (with inversion)\n💡 Fronting draws attention to the moved element!\n\n- **Inversion for Emphasis:** Changing subject-verb order after negative or limiting adverbs.\nNever: Never have I seen such a beautiful place. (Not: Never I have seen)\nRarely: Rarely does she miss class.\nSeldom: Seldom do we get such opportunities.\nNot only: Not only did he pass, but he also got the highest marks.\nHardly/Scarcely: Hardly had the exam started when the power went out.\n💡 Negative adverbs at the start require inversion!\n\n- **Passive Voice for Emphasis:** Emphasizes the action or object, de-emphasizes the agent.\nActive: The government built the Padma Bridge.\nPassive (Emphasis on Bridge): The Padma Bridge was built by the government.\nPassive (Agent omitted): The Padma Bridge was built in 2022.\nWhen to Use: Focus on action/object, agent unknown or obvious\n💡 Passive = object becomes more important!\n\n- **Double Emphasis (It is...that + Cleft):** Combining techniques for maximum emphasis.\nVery Strong Emphasis: It was because of hard work that she succeeded.\nNormal: She succeeded because of hard work.\nDouble Cleft: What it was that helped her was hard work.\n💡 Use sparingly in academic writing!\n\n- **'There' Structures:** Emphasizes existence or presence.\nNormal: Many students are in the library.\nEmphasis: There are many students in the library.\nNormal: A problem exists with the system.\nEmphasis: There is a problem with the system.\n💡 'There' + be introduces new information!\n\n- **End-Weight (Heavy Information Last):** Place complex/long information at the end.\n❌ Awkward: That you passed the HSC exam pleases me.\n✅ Better: It pleases me that you passed the HSC exam.\n❌ Awkward: To study hard for HSC is necessary.\n✅ Better: It is necessary to study hard for HSC.\n💡 Short before long = natural rhythm!\n\n\n## 2. 💡 Practical Examples (বাস্তব উদাহরণসমূহ)\n\n1. It-Cleft: It was in 1971 that Bangladesh gained independence.\n(Emphasizes the year 1971)\n\n2. What-Cleft: What Bangladesh needs is better infrastructure for education.\n(Emphasizes 'better infrastructure')\n\n3. Fronting: In Dhaka, traffic is a major challenge for daily commuters.\n(Emphasizes location 'Dhaka')\n\n4. Inversion: Never have I experienced such hospitality as in Bangladesh.\n(Emphasizes 'never' - strong statement)\n\n5. Passive: The Sundarbans is recognized as a UNESCO World Heritage Site.\n(Emphasizes the Sundarbans, not who recognized it)\n\n6. Double Emphasis: It was through dedication that what she achieved was success.\n(Very strong emphasis on dedication and success)\n\n7. There-Structure: There are millions of students preparing for HSC in Bangladesh.\n(Introduces new information about students)\n\n8. End-Weight: It is important for HSC students to maintain a consistent study schedule.\n(Complex information moved to end)\n\n\n## 3. ✍️ Practice Drills (অনুশীলনমূলক কাজ)\n\n- **Exercise 1:** Rewrite with emphasis: Rina won the competition. (Use It is...who)\n\n- **Exercise 2:** Rewrite: She needs practice. (Use What...is)\n\n- **Exercise 3:** Rewrite with inversion: I have never seen such beauty.\n\n- **Exercise 4:** Change to passive: The teacher explained the lesson clearly.\n\n- **Exercise 5:** Rewrite: Many people live in Dhaka. (Use There...)\n\n- **Exercise 6:** Improve: To pass HSC is her goal. (Use It...to)",
