      "order_index": 1,
      "content_text": "# Reported Speech (Advanced)\n\n**Level**: Class 11-12 | **Topic**: Reported Speech: Advanced (উন্নত ন্যারেশন ও রিপোর্টিং)\n\n\n## 1. 📖 Grammar Rules & Explanations (নিয়মাবলী ও ব্যাখ্যা)\n\n- **🎓 Advanced Reported Speech:** Master the art of reporting what others said for HSC! Learn complex reporting patterns beyond basic rules.\n\n- **Advanced Reporting Verbs:** Use specific verbs to show how something was said.\nSay/Tell: Basic reporting\n- She said (that) she was tired. / She told me (that) she was tired.\nOther Verbs: Explain, mention, complain, suggest, promise, warn, remind, advise\n- He suggested (that) I study harder. (suggestion)\n- She warned me not to be late. (warning)\n- They promised to help. (promise)\n💡 Different verbs show different intentions!\n\n- **Mixed Tenses in Reported Speech:** When the reporting verb is present or the statement is still true.\nPresent Reporting Verb: No tense change needed\n- She says she is tired. (says = present, so 'is' stays)\n- He tells me he likes Dhaka. (tells = present)\nUniversal Truth: No tense change\n- The teacher said, \"The sun rises in the east.\"\n- The teacher said (that) the sun rises in the east. (still true)\n💡 Universal truths keep present tense!\n\n- **Reporting Questions (Advanced Patterns):** Yes/No Questions: Use if/whether\n- \"Do you like Dhaka?\" → He asked if/whether I liked Dhaka.\nWh- Questions: Use the question word\n- \"Where do you live?\" → She asked where I lived.\nIndirect Questions: Polite questions already in statement form\n- \"Could you tell me what time it is?\" → He asked what time it was.\n💡 No question mark in reported questions!\n\n- **Reporting Commands and Requests:** Commands: tell/order + someone + (not) to + verb\n- \"Study hard!\" → The teacher told us to study hard.\n- \"Don't be late!\" → She warned us not to be late.\nRequests: ask + someone + (not) to + verb\n- \"Please help me.\" → He asked me to help him.\n- \"Could you open the door?\" → She asked me to open the door.\n💡 Use 'to + infinitive' for commands/requests!\n\n- **Reporting with Modal Verbs (Advanced):** Shifting Modals:\n- Will → would: \"I will come\" → He said he would come.\n- Can → could: \"I can do it\" → She said she could do it.\n- May → might: \"It may rain\" → He said it might rain.\nNo Change for: would, could, should, might, ought to, used to\n- \"I would like tea\" → He said he would like tea.\n💡 Some modals don't change in reported speech!\n\n- **Reporting Suggestions and Advice:** Suggest: suggest + that + subject + (should) + verb OR suggest + -ing\n- \"Let's go to Dhaka.\" → He suggested (that) we go/should go to Dhaka.\n- He suggested going to Dhaka.\nAdvise/Recommend: advise/recommend + (that) + subject + (should) + verb\n- \"You should study more.\" → She advised (that) I study/should study more.\n💡 'Should' is often optional in these patterns!\n\n- **Reporting with Special Verbs:** Admit, Deny: admit/deny + -ing\n- \"Yes, I broke the rule.\" → He admitted breaking the rule.\n- \"I didn't cheat.\" → She denied cheating on the exam.\nApologize, Blame: apologize for / blame someone for + -ing\n- \"Sorry for being late.\" → He apologized for being late.\n- \"You caused the problem.\" → He blamed me for causing the problem.\n💡 These verbs use gerund (-ing form)!\n\n- **Complex Reported Speech - Mixed Structures:** Reporting Long Conversations: Use multiple clauses\n- She explained that she had been studying for hours and that she needed rest.\nReporting with Reasons: Include explanatory clauses\n- He said he couldn't come because he was busy with HSC preparation.\nReporting Consequences: Use result clauses\n- She mentioned that if she studied hard, she would pass.\n💡 Link multiple ideas with 'that', 'and', 'because'!\n\n\n## 2. 💡 Practical Examples (বাস্তব উদাহরণসমূহ)\n\n1. Reporting Verb: She complained that the HSC syllabus was too difficult.\n(Direct: \"The HSC syllabus is too difficult.\")\n\n2. Mixed Tense: He says he is preparing for the HSC examination this year.\n(Says = present, so 'is' doesn't change)\n\n3. Universal Truth: The teacher said water boils at 100 degrees Celsius.\n(Still true, tense doesn't change)\n\n4. Question: She asked if I had visited the Padma Bridge last summer.\n(Direct: \"Did you visit the Padma Bridge last summer?\")\n\n5. Command: The teacher ordered us not to use phones during the exam.\n(Direct: \"Don't use phones during the exam!\")\n\n6. Request: He asked me to help him with mathematics homework.\n(Direct: \"Please help me with my mathematics homework.\")\n\n7. Suggestion: She suggested that we visit the Sundarbans during vacation.\n(Direct: \"Let's visit the Sundarbans during vacation.\")\n\n8. Admission: He admitted making a mistake in the HSC application form.\n(Direct: \"Yes, I made a mistake in the HSC application form.\")\n\n\n## 3. ✍️ Practice Drills (অনুশীলনমূলক কাজ)\n\n- **Exercise 1:** Report: \"I am studying for HSC,\" she said. (Use 'told me')\n\n- **Exercise 2:** Report: \"Do you like mathematics?\" he asked. (Use 'if')\n\n- **Exercise 3:** Report: \"Study hard!\" the teacher said. (Use 'told us')\n\n- **Exercise 4:** Report: \"Please help me,\" she requested. (Use 'asked me')\n\n- **Exercise 5:** Report: \"Let's go to Dhaka,\" he suggested. (Use 'suggested')\n\n- **Exercise 6:** Report: \"I broke the window,\" he admitted. (Use 'admitted')",
